n(n-1) ]\n\nSo ( (sum i)^2 - sum i^2 ) =22, n(n-1)=6. So 22/6=11/3. Which matches.\n\nBut when trying to derive the formula as:\n\nTotal sum / number of products = [ (H_n)^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] / [C(n,2)]\n\nBut since (sum i )=H_n, sum i^2=H_n^{(2)}\n\nThus, substituting:\n\n= [ H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] / [C(n,2) ]\n\nBut for this to equal [ (sum i)^2 - sum i^2 ] / [n(n-1) ]\n\nWhich for n=3 is 22 /6=11/3, which matches the direct calculation.\n\nThus, the formula is correct. So why was I getting a different answer? Because perhaps I confused the sum of products with something else.\n\nHence, the arithmetic mean is indeed [ (H_n)^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] / [C(n,2) ]\n\nBut then simplifying:\n\n [H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] / [ n(n-1)/2 ]= 2 [H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] / [n(n-1)]\n\nWhich, for n=3, gives 2*(11)/9≈22/9≈2.444..., which does not match the arithmetic mean. Wait, wait, this is conflicting. What\'s H_n and H_n^{(2)} for n=3:\n\nH_3=6, sum of squares H_3^{(2)}=14. Thus,\n\n [6^2 -14]/ [3*2/1]= 22 /3=7.333..., which does not make sense.\n\nWait, no, that can\'t be. Wait, the arithmetic mean cannot be 7.333... if the sum is 11 and number of products is 3. So where is this confusion coming from.\n\nWait, no: The arithmetic mean is 11 divided by 3=11/3≈3.666... So perhaps I mixed up the numerator and denominator.\n\nAh, now I realize the error: when we compute the formula [ (H_n)^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] / [something], are we correctly computing?\n\nWait, [ (H_n)^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] is equal to 2 sum_{i < j}ij, right?\n\nFrom the identity:\n\n(sum i)^2 = sum i^2 + 2 sum_{i < j} ij → sum_{i < j} ij = ( (sum i)^2 - sum i^2 ) / 2 = (H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)}) /2.\n\nSo sum_{i < j}ij= (H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)}) /2.\n\nThus, the sum is (H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)}) /2.\n\nTherefore, the arithmetic mean is (sum of products) / [n(n-1)/2].\n\nBut (sum of products) is (H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)}) /2.\n\nTherefore, arithmetic mean = [ (H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)}) /2 ] / [n(n-1)/2 ] = (H_n^2 - H_n^{(2)}) /n(n-1).\n\nSimplifies to [ (H_n)^2 - H_n^{(2)} ] / [ n(n